    steps: [
      { rail: 'Overview', tag: 'PROBLEM', title: 'Who runs this in production?',
        body: ['Across streaming, visual commerce, legal tech, logistics and fintech, leading enterprises have converged on **six reusable patterns**.', 'Each one starts from a bottleneck that closed APIs could not solve.'],
        key: 'Bottleneck, blueprint, result.', chips: ['8 companies', '6 industries', '?'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']] },
      { rail: 'Netflix', tag: 'PATTERN 1', title: 'Prefill-only catalog ranker',
        body: ['**[Netflix GenRec](' + U.netflix + ').** Autoregressive decoding is too slow for real-time catalog ranking across millions of concurrent users.', 'Verbalize user history into text and attach a **linear catalog scoring head**, served on vLLM in prefill-only mode (`output_tokens: 0`).'],
        key: 'A statistically significant engagement lift across about 10% of global Netflix traffic, at sub-second latency.', chips: ['Netflix', 'Media & streaming', '~10% of global traffic'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']], labels: { b: 'Decoding\ntoo slow', p: 'Prefill-only\nscoring head', r: 'Engagement\nlift' } },
      { rail: 'Pinterest', tag: 'PATTERN 2', title: 'Surgical vision encoder graft',
        body: ['**[Pinterest](' + U.pinterest + ').** Closed multimodal APIs are too expensive at high discovery QPS and lack proprietary catalog context.', 'Post-train an open VLM and **replace its generic vision tower with Pinterest\'s pre-computed visual embeddings**.'],
        key: '90% lower serving cost than closed APIs, with higher visual search relevance.', chips: ['Pinterest', 'Visual commerce', '90% lower cost'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']], labels: { b: 'APIs too\nexpensive', p: 'Graft own\nvision tower', r: '90% lower\ncost' } },
      { rail: 'Harvey', tag: 'PATTERN 3', title: 'Matter-isolated rubric gates',
        body: ['**[Harvey](' + U.harvey + ').** Global law firms need strict client-matter isolation and adherence to complex partner rubrics.', 'Post-train open weights across **75,000+ legal rubric criteria** and enforce pre-submission harness verification gates.'],
        key: 'Nearly 2× strict all-pass rate, with +16.7pp from harness gates alone.', chips: ['Harvey', 'Legal & advisory', '~2× all-pass rate'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']], labels: { b: 'Isolation\n+ rubrics', p: 'Rubric-trained\n+ gates', r: '~2× all-pass\nrate' } },
      { rail: 'DoorDash', tag: 'PATTERN 4', title: 'Teacher-student SKU distillation',
        body: ['**[DoorDash](' + U.doordash + ').** Labeling millions of catalog SKUs and triaging defect photos on frontier APIs is too slow and costly.', 'Use a frontier teacher (Gemini 3.1 Pro) to label domain pairs, then distill into **quantized LoRA/QLoRA open student models on Ray**.'],
        key: '10× lower serving cost at under 150 ms TTFT. Training turnaround cut from weeks to days.', chips: ['DoorDash', 'Logistics & retail', '10× lower cost'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']], labels: { b: 'Labeling\nat scale', p: 'Teacher →\nstudent', r: '10× cheaper\n<150 ms' } },
      { rail: 'Coinbase', tag: 'PATTERN 5', title: 'Sub-$100 verifiable RL and gateway',
        body: ['**[Coinbase](' + U.coinbase + ') and [Uber](' + U.uber + ').** Frontier APIs miss domain fraud patterns and cannot receive unredacted customer PII at high QPS.', 'Post-train a **9B open model with single-GPU GRPO for under $100** (Coinbase), behind a PII-redacting GenAI Gateway handling 16M queries a month (Uber).'],
        key: '+9.6% F1 over frontier APIs, 55% lower latency, and 30% less live production fraud.', chips: ['Coinbase · Uber', 'Fintech & platforms', '+9.6% F1'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']], labels: { b: 'Fraud + PII\nlimits', p: '<$100 GRPO\n+ gateway', r: '+9.6% F1\n−30% fraud' } },
      { rail: 'Amazon', tag: 'PATTERN 6', title: 'Decision-model swap for labeling',
        body: ['**[Amazon](' + U.strands + ') and [Microsoft](' + U.msga + ').** Claude Sonnet 4.6 labeled tickets against a fixed 20-category catalog at very high volume, generating JSON and reasoning on every ticket.',
          'Amazon replaced it with a self-hosted **decision model** (Strands Decider E4B, a Gemma 4 build) that returns a probability per label. A three-way config switch ran both models on live tickets before the cut-over.',
          'Microsoft reports **4,000+ customers** on its Decision model in just over five weeks, for routing, support triage, tagging and classification. Early adopters include Epic, Equinor, Atos Group and Raiffeisen Bank.'],
        key: '98% lower cost and 78% less time per call, with labels as good as the production LLM.', chips: ['Amazon · Microsoft', 'Internal ops & platforms', '98% lower cost'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']], labels: { b: 'LLM labels\nevery ticket', p: 'Decision\nmodel', r: '98% cheaper\n78% faster' } },
      { rail: 'Recap', tag: 'RECAP', title: 'Six patterns to reuse',
        body: ['Score instead of generate. Graft your own encoder. Gate on rubrics. Distill from a frontier teacher. Post-train cheaply behind a gateway. Swap an LLM for a decision model on closed choices.', 'All six depend on holding the weights.'],
        key: 'Next: the napkin math that decides where to run them.', chips: ['8 companies', '6 industries', '6 patterns'], show: ['b', 'p', 'r'], edges: [['b', 'p'], ['p', 'r']] },
    ],
